  "finding_count": 2,
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops",
      "evidence": "paper.tex Method Design rationale (cycle overshoot \"exactly ln 4 \u2248 1.39 at every n\"); closed_form_gap.json log_overshoot.cycle = 1.3863 for all n; claims_grounding.json has no claim for this statement.",
      "fix": "Drop \"only\" and the bottleneck characterisation. For example: \"the bound is tight up to a constant factor on some graphs such as paths, cycles and lollipops, and overshoots by a factor that grows with n on dense graphs (Section~\\ref{sec:rationale}).\"",
      "id": "intro-bound-tightness-1",
      "issue": "No grounded claim supports the word \"only\". It conflicts with the paper's own closed-form analysis, where the cycle (not a bottlenecked graph) has a constant overshoot of exactly ln 4, so the bound is tight up to a factor of 4 at every n.",
      "section": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "claim": "the clipped log error saturates at 10, which would hide very large failures. Per-graph errors were not retained, so whether any test graph approaches that cap cannot be checked here.",
      "evidence": "paper.tex Results section \"What the evidence does not show\" (no single graph exceeds about 4.35); claims_grounding.json claim[8] (derived from final.metric.macro_log_mae); protocol_facts.json ERR_CLIP=10; evaluate_final.py macro_error clips at ERR_CLIP.",
      "fix": "Replace the sentence with a statement that the clip at 10 cannot have been binding, since the macro value bounds every graph's error at about 4.35 (factor about 78). Keep the limitation that per-graph and per-family errors were not retained.",
      "id": "clip-cap-inconsistency-1",
      "issue": "The paper's own grounded derivation shows that a family-macro of 0.181 limits any single graph's log error to at most 24 x 0.181 \u2248 4.35, which is below the clip of 10. The clip therefore cannot have been active, and it cannot hide very large failures in this result. The limitation as written contradicts the earlier worst-case-concentration statement.",
      "section": "Discussion and Limitations (Evaluation scope and label noise)"
    }
  ],
